{% sec 1, "A Stone in a Pond", "What happens after the central bank changes its rate?", "A stone in a pond" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Drop a stone into a pond. The splash is instant, the first ripple spreads out in a second, and it takes a while before the ripples reach the far bank. A change in the central bank's rate spreads the same way. The route it takes is called the {% gl "transmission mechanism" %}transmission mechanism{% endgl %}.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The splash (same day):</strong> financial markets. Overnight rates, short-dated government bonds and the currency reprice within minutes.</li>
      <li><strong>The first ripples (weeks to months):</strong> banks change the rates on loans and savings; house prices, share prices and lending conditions start to adjust.</li>
      <li><strong>The far bank (6 to 24 months):</strong> people and businesses change how much they spend, invest and hire. Last of all, inflation moves.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Follow a rate rise</h3><p>Step through a quarter-point rise from the day of the decision to two years later. The lit area shows how far the ripple has spread; each dot is one stage, in the part of the economy it reaches.</p></div>
